#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
atui-remote -- the telnet bridge used to drive a remote atui target.

atui targets machines that cannot show us their screen: OS/2 over telnet today,
DOS and other serial/console sessions tomorrow. tui-test already renders a
terminal, waits on it, searches it and clicks it, so the bridge is a telnet
client that runs as the program tui-test starts:

    tui-test run --cols 80 --rows 25 -- tools/atui-remote --host 10.196.88.129

Every tui-test verb (text, find, expect, cells, key, type, resize, screenshot)
then acts on the remote screen, and there is still only one screen model in the
system -- the emulator's. A second "expected screen" here would mean testing the
model instead of the application.

Why a program at all, when macOS used to ship telnet: it no longer does, and
`nc` does not speak RFC 854, so negotiation never happens and a full-screen
program cannot work. What this adds over a socket is what a TUI depends on:

  * negotiation answered, never invented -- see Negotiator;
  * NAWS, so the target is told the size tui-test gave the session, and told
    again on `tui-test resize`;
  * echo owned by exactly one side, taken from what the server negotiates rather
    than guessed, because a doubled echo corrupts every screen assertion;
  * character set translation (OS/2 speaks code page 437, an emulator expects
    UTF-8), which is what makes box drawing legible;
  * login automation, so a session starts at a prompt;
  * byte-exact logging of both directions, because "the screen is wrong" is only
    diagnosable from the bytes.

Subcommands:
    (none)        connect and relay -- this is what tui-test runs
    serve         a local fake target, so the bridge itself has a test subject
    bridge-test   local acceptance suite (needs tui-test)
    probe         ask a real target the questions the port depends on
    put / get     move one file over FTP
    sync          mirror the sources onto the target, by hash
    build         sync, run the makefile there, bring the log back and report
                  its status as this command's exit status

Stdlib only, and it is development tooling: nothing in src/ refers to it, and it
is never compiled or shipped.
"""

